<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Quiz 3</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Answer all questions. Show your work.</alert>
        Clearly write your name on the top of the page.
        Differentiate each of the following.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise workspace="1in"> 
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(x) = 2.5 x^2 - x + 4.2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We will use the power rule to answer this.
          <md>
            f'(x) = (2.5)2x - 1 + 0.
          </md>
          That is,
          <md>
            f'(x) = 5x -1.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>f(t) = t^4 \cos(t) </m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          To differentiate we will use the product rule.
          Note that
          <md>
            f(t) = h(t)g(t)
          </md>
          where <m>h(t)=t^4</m> and <m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m>.
          Hence,
          <md>
            f'(t) = h'(t)g(t) + h(t)g'(t).
          </md>
          Using the power rule,
          <m>
            h'(t) = 4t^3.
          </m>
          Also, <m>g'(t)=-\sin(t)</m>.
          Hence,
          <md>
            f'(t) = 4t^3 \cos(t) + t^4(-\sin(t)).
          </md>
          That is,
          <md>
            f'(t) = 4t^3 \cos(t) - t^4\sin(t).
          </md>
          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>


    <exercise workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>F(y) = \left(\frac{1}{y^2}-\frac{1}{y^4}\right)(y + 8y^3).</m>    
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Note that
          <md>
            <mrow>F(y) \amp = \left(\frac{1}{y^2}-\frac{1}{y^4}\right)(y + 8y^3) = \frac{1}{y}+8y-\frac{1}{y^3} - \frac{8}{y}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 8y - y^{-3} - 7y^{-1}. </mrow>
          </md>
          Hence, using the power rule,
          <md>
            F'(y) = 8 + 3 y^{-4} + 7y^{-2} = 8 + \frac{3}{y^4} + \frac{7}{y^2}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>

      <solution>
        <p>
          We can also answer this question using the product rule.
          Let
          <md>
            h(y) = \frac{1}{y^2}-\frac{1}{y^4} \text{ and } g(y) = y + 8y^3.
          </md>
          Then, <m>f'(y)=h'(y)g(y)+h(y)g'(y).</m>
          Using the power rule to calculate each of <m>h'(y)</m> and <m>g'(y)</m>, we get
          <md>
            <mrow>f'(y) \amp= \left(\frac{-2}{y^3} + \frac{4}{y^5}\right) ( y+8y^3 ) +  \left(\frac{1}{y^2}-\frac{1}{y^4}\right)(1 + 24y^2)</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{-2}{y^2} -16 + \frac{4}{y^4} +\frac{32}{y^2} + \frac{1}{y^2} +24 -\frac{1}{y^4} - \frac{24}{y^2} </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{7}{y^2} + 8 + \frac{3}{y^4}. </mrow>
          </md>
          Note that we get the same answer, no matter how we differentiate <m>f(y)</m>.
          However, using the power rule in Solution 1 was simpler.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    </page>
    <page>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>f(x) = \sin(x^2+1).</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
      
    <solution>
      <p>
        To answer this we will use the chain rule.
        We can write <m>f(x)</m> as <m>f(x) = h(g(x))</m> where <m>h(x) = \sin(x)</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2 +1.</m>
        By the chain rule,
        <md>
          f'(x) = h'(g(x))\cdot g'(x).
        </md>
        Thus,
        <md>
          f'(x) = \cos(x^2 + 1) (2x).
        </md>
        That is,
        <md>
          f'(x) = 2x \cos(x^2 + 1).
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m> h(r) = \frac{r}{\sqrt{r^2+2}}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        We will use the quotient rule here.
        <md>
          h(r) = \frac{f(r)}{g(r)}
        </md>
        for <m>f(r) = r</m> and <m>g(r) = \sqrt{r^2 + 2}.</m>
        Thus,
        <md>
          <mrow> h'(r) \amp = \frac{f'(r)g(r) - f(r)g'(r)}{[g(r)]^2} </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp \frac{(1)\sqrt{r^2 + 2} - r\left(\frac{1}{2}(r^2 + 2)^{-1/2}(2r) \right)}{r^2 + 2}</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \frac{(r^2 + 2)-r^2}{(r^2 + 2)^{\frac{3}{2}}} </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \frac{2}{(r^2 + 2)^{\frac{3}{2}}}.</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We can also answer this question using the product rule. Note that
        <md>
          h(r) = r (r^2+2)^{-1/2}.
        </md>
        Hence
        <md>
          <mrow>h'(r) \amp = (1)(r^2+2)^{-1/2} + r \left(\frac{-1}{2}(r^2+2)^{-3/2}(2r) \right) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt{r^2+2}} - \frac{r^2}{(r^2+2)^{3/2}}</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp = \frac{2}{(r^2 + 2)^{\frac{3}{2}}}.</mrow>
        </md>
        
        
      </p>
    </solution>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>